_reruns": ["Add a selfcheck case exercising executor._limit_price() with a Rule A order where ref_price is left stale (= old entry_anchor_price) to make visible how much of the ceiling raise is actually usable — currently H1-H4 only test ref_price already set to the new ceiling.", "Before any live plan uses ceiling_rule=A, verify DollarBill's plan-generation path actually re-derives ref_price/qty per the CLI's ref_price_de_xuat output — a dry-run on one real LAG book with --apply, followed by a diff review, would confirm this isn't just documented but actually done."], "summary": "Every artifact the finding cites checks out: the code (no_chase_ceiling.py, the plan.py diff, lag_rule_a_ceiling.py) matches the described mechanism exactly, the selfcheck runs green live with numbers matching the commit message verbatim, an independent BigQuery recompute reproduces all four headline ceiling values to the digit, no live plan currently carries ceiling_rule (confirmed by direct file scan), the executor is genuinely untouched, and the user-decision correction on the bus carries a direct user quote rather than being a self-serving reinterpretation. The one real gap is that the executor's chase-cap formula uses ref_price independently of the new hard ceiling, so unless ref_price is manually re-derived when Rule A is applied, the ~16bps insurance premium this whole change exists to justify may buy nothing — a risk the finding discloses but does not yet test or enforce. This is an operational follow-up, not a defect in what was built, so the finding stands as [REDACTED]D."}
