852:- [[REDACTED]T01:16:53Z] quant-skeptic/verification — ✅ [REDACTED]VERIFY: wire Rule A tran gia mua (LAG entry-window) — code + selfcheck xong, CHUA ap live: {"finding_topic": "wire Rule A tran gia mua (LAG entry-window) — code + selfcheck xong, CHUA ap live", "verdict": "[REDACTED]D", "confidence": "high", "checks": {"look_ahead_leak": "pass — anchor must be a session strictly before plan_date (invariant #4), enforced in _verify_rule_a() and covered by selfcheck D10/D11/F5 (anchor_date=plan_date and anchor_date>plan_date both fail-closed). No forward columns (profit_*/O1W../*_center_*) touched; lag_rule_a_ceiling.py reads tav2_bq.ticker.Price only for sessions < plan_date and hard-rejects if BQ hasn't loaded the prior session yet (age-gate).", "oos_robustness": "na — this is an execution-policy ceiling, not an alpha signal; no NAV series exists to walk-forward. The underlying README explicitly states DSR/PBO don't apply and forbids citing any number as an edge — finding respects this.", "panel_curation_bias": "pass — scope was measured, not asserted: grep-verified across 97 historical plan files that entry_anchor_price only ever appears in LAG book orders (DRI/POW/SCL/SSI, 08-10); BAL/CAPIT/momentum genuinely 0 hits. Research basket (30 tickers) mixes 23 pre-cached thin names + 7 freshly-pulled liquid names (FPT/MBB/ACB/HDB/DCM/VGC/PVT), not cherry-picked winners.", "reproducibility_selfcheck": "pass — ran rule_a_ceiling_selfcheck.py myself: all 8 groups (A-H) PASS, including 'G1 nạp lại 96 plan thật, 23 lệnh mua có trần — 0 lệnh đổi giá trị', matching the commit message and payload exactly. Independently recomputed the 4 headline ceiling values via a fresh bq query (DRI/POW/SCL/SSI @[REDACTED] Price) — floor(price×1.03) reproduced 13596/14008/24926/25183 exactly.", "param_overfit": "pass — τ=3% is a fixed prior carried over from the [REDACTED] discretionary-class tradeoff-curve breakpoint decision, not fit on this study's data; this study only varies size/κ/lag as sensitivity checks around the fixed policy choice, and reports 9/9 direction-consistent cells rather than a single tuned point.", "capacity_adv_realism": "na/pass — not a capacity-dependent edge; ceiling formula is a pure price-anchor rule independent of order size. The companion capacity finding (pacing/participation) is correctly kept separate and NO-GO'd in the same README.", "arithmetic_mechanism": "pass with a flagged gap — the 4 ceiling recomputations check out exactly (see independent_recompute), the fail-closed/fail-open matrix is internally consistent, and executor.py is genuinely untouched (git show 2db6d37 -- trading_bot/executor.py is empty). BUT: executor cap = min(ref_price×(1+chase%), q.ceiling, hard_ceiling) — if ref_price is not raised alongside Rule A's ceiling, ref_price×(1+chase%) can silently dominate and neutralize most of Rule A's effect. The finding discloses this risk explicitly but no selfcheck exercises the stale-ref_price + Rule A combination end-to-end."}, "independent_recompute": "Re-ran rule_a_ceiling_selfcheck.py directly (96 plans / 23 ceiled orders / 0 changed, all PASS) and independently queried tav2_bq.ticker.Price for DRI/POW/SCL/SSI on [REDACTED] to recompute floor(price×1.03): got 13596/14008/24926/25183, exactly matching the four headline numbers in the finding. Both matched to full precision.", "killer_objection": "hard_no_chase_ceiling_vnd is correctly raised and fail-closed, but executor._limit_price()'s cap = min(ref_price×(1+chase%), q.ceiling, hard) means the policy benefit evaporates silently unless ref_price is also updated when Rule A is applied. lag_rule_a_ceiling.py's --apply intentionally does NOT touch ref_price/qty (by design, sizing is DollarBill/user's job), so whether the ~16bps insurance premium is actually paid for anything real depends entirely on a manual downstream step (re-deriving ref_price/qty) that has zero code-level enforcement and zero selfcheck coverage of the combined scenario.", "recommended_reruns": ["Add a selfcheck case exercising executor._limit_price() with a Rule A order where ref_price is left stale (= old entry_anchor_price) to make visible how much of the ceiling raise is actually usable — currently H1-H4 only test ref_price already set to the new ceiling.", "Before any live plan uses ceiling_rule=A, verify DollarBill's plan-generation path actually re-derives ref_price/qty per the CLI's ref_price_de_xuat output — a dry-run on one real LAG book with --apply, followed by a diff review, would confirm this isn't just documented but actually done."], "summary": "Every artifact the finding cites checks out: the code (no_chase_ceiling.py, the plan.py diff, lag_rule_a_ceiling.py) matches the described mechanism exactly, the selfcheck runs green live with numbers matching the commit message verbatim, an independent BigQuery recompute reproduces all four headline ceiling values to the digit, no live plan currently carries ceiling_rule (confirmed by direct file scan), the executor is genuinely untouched, and the user-decision correction on the bus carries a direct user quote rather than being a self-serving reinterpretation. The one real gap is that the executor's chase-cap formula uses ref_price independently of the new hard ceiling, so unless ref_price is manually re-derived when Rule A is applied, the ~16bps insurance premium this whole change exists to justify may buy nothing — a risk the finding discloses but does not yet test or enforce. This is an operational follow-up, not a defect in what was built, so the finding stands as [REDACTED]D."}
