852:- [[REDACTED]T01:16:53Z] quant-skeptic/verification — ✅ [REDACTED]VERIFY: wire Rule A tran gia mua (LAG entry-window) — code + selfcheck xong, CHUA ap live: {"finding_topic": "wire Rule A tran gia mua (LAG entry-window) — code + selfcheck xong, CHUA ap live", "verdict": "[REDACTED]D", "confidence": "high", "checks": {"look_ahead_leak": "pass — anchor must be a session strictly before plan_date (invariant #4), enforced in _verify_rule_a() and covered by selfcheck D10/D11/F5 (anchor_date=plan_date and anchor_date>plan_date both fail-closed). No forward columns (profit_*/O1W../*_center_*) touched; lag_rule_a_ceiling.py reads tav2_bq.ticker.Price only for sessions < plan_date and hard-rejects if BQ hasn't loaded the prior session yet (age-gate).", "oos_robustness": "na — this is an execution-policy ceiling, not an alpha signal; no NAV series exists to walk-forward. The underlying README explicitly states DSR/PBO don't apply and forbids citing any number as an edge — finding respects this.", "panel_curation_bias": "pass — scope was measured, not asserted: grep-verified across 97 historical plan files that entry_anchor_price only ever appears in LAG book orders (DRI/POW/SCL/SSI, 08-10); BAL/CAPIT/momentum genuinely 0 hits. Research basket (30 tickers) mixes 23 pre-cached thin names + 7 freshly-pulled liquid names (FPT/MBB/ACB/HDB/DCM/VGC/PVT), not cherry-picked winners.", "reproducibility_selfcheck": "pass — ran rule_a_ceiling_selfcheck.py myself: all 8 groups (A-H) PASS, including 'G1 nạp lại 96 plan thật, 23 lệnh mua có trần — 0 lệnh đổi giá trị', matching the commit message and payload exactly. Independently recomputed the 4 headline ceiling values via a fresh bq query (DRI/POW/SCL/SSI @[REDACTED] Price) — floor(price×1.03) reproduced 13596/14008/24926/25183 exactly.", "param_overfit": "pass — τ=3% is a fixed prior carried over from the [REDACTED] discretionary-class tradeoff-curve breakpoint decision, not fit on this study's data; this study only varies size/κ/lag as sensitivity checks around the fixed policy choice, and reports 9/9 direction-consistent cells rather than a single tuned point.", "capacity_adv_realism": "na/pass — not a capacity-dependent edge; ceiling formula is a pure price-anchor rule independent of order size. The companion capacity finding (pacing/participation) is correctly kept separate and NO-GO'd in the same README.", "arithmetic_mechanism": "pass with a flagged gap — the 4 ceiling recomputations check out exactly (see independent_recompute), the fail-closed/fail-open matrix is internally consistent, and executor.py is genuinely untouched (git show 2db6d37 -- trading_bot/executor.py is empty). BUT: executor cap = min(ref_price×(1+chase%), q.ceiling, hard_ceiling) — if ref_price is not raised alongside Rule A's ceiling, ref_price×(1+chase%) can silently dominate and neutralize most of Rule A's effect. The finding discloses this risk explicitly but no selfcheck exercises the stale-ref_price + Rule A combination end-to-end."}, "independent_recompute": "Re-ran rule_a_ceiling_selfcheck.py directly (96 plans / 23 ceiled orders / 0 changed, all PASS) and independently queried tav2_bq.ticker.Price for DRI/POW/SCL/SSI on [REDACTED] to recompute floor(price×1.03): got 13596/14008/24926/25183, exactly matching the four headline numbers in the finding. Both matched to full precision.", "killer_objection": "hard_no_chase_ceiling_vnd is correctly raised and fail-closed, but executor._limit_price()'s cap = min(ref_price×(1+chase%), q.ceiling, hard) means the policy benefit evaporates silently unless ref_price is also updated when Rule A is applied. lag_rule_a_ceiling.py's --apply intentionally does NOT touch ref_price/qty (by design, sizing is DollarBill/user's job), so whether the ~16bps insurance premium is actually paid for anything real depends entirely on a manual downstream step (re-deriving ref_price/qty) that has zero code-level enforcement and zero selfcheck coverage of the combined scenario.", "recommended